# G012 — Distance Between Two Cities

*v2. See `distance-cities.md` for prose: first encounter with world-
state, fact lookup as a resolver primitive alongside computation,
representation, and scheduling. Computed values vs looked-up values
differ architecturally.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Warehouse Proximity Selection

[warehouse-dispatch @customer-address
    IN ORDER TO
        resolve the customer destination and three candidate warehouses
        compute great-circle distances concurrently
        and dispatch to the nearest warehouse

    @dest <- [city[lookup {address: @customer-address}]]

    concurrent [
        tokyo:  @w1 <- [warehouse[coords {name: "warehouse_tokyo"}]]
    |
        sydney: @w2 <- [warehouse[coords {name: "warehouse_sydney"}]]
    |
        cairo:  @w3 <- [warehouse[coords {name: "warehouse_cairo"}]]
    ]
    join

    concurrent [
        @d1 <- [haversine {lat1: @dest:lat, lon1: @dest:lon,
                            lat2: @w1:lat,   lon2: @w1:lon}]
    |
        @d2 <- [haversine {lat1: @dest:lat, lon1: @dest:lon,
                            lat2: @w2:lat,   lon2: @w2:lon}]
    |
        @d3 <- [haversine {lat1: @dest:lat, lon1: @dest:lon,
                            lat2: @w3:lat,   lon2: @w3:lon}]
    ]
    join

    @nearest <- select min over [(@w1, @d1), (@w2, @d2), (@w3, @d3)]
    [dispatch {warehouse: @nearest, destination: @dest}]
]

---

## Verb Surface

[haversine
    {lat1, lon1, lat2, lon2}                 ;; -> km
]

[city
    lookup              {name}                ;; -> {lat, lon} | #unknown-city
]

[city-distance
    {from, to}                                 ;; -> km (chains lookup + haversine)
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: fact lookup `where` world-state is external

[city[lookup {name: @n}]
    IN ORDER TO
        return coordinates from a shared mutable fact store
        not from first-principles computation
        because city locations are contingent facts, not derivable theorems

    @n in fact-store (database or hardcoded table)
        where present     -> return {lat, lon}
        where absent       -> #unknown-city
]

Computed values are deterministic and timeless; looked-up values are
contingent and mutable. The resolver must treat them differently —
caches differ, freshness semantics differ. G012 is the first
encounter with world-state as a resolver primitive.

---

## Demo

[demo
    -> [haversine {lat1: 40.7128, lon1: -74.0060,
                    lat2: 51.5074, lon2: -0.1278}]
    ;; -> 5570.25 km (NYC to London)

    -> [city-distance {from: "Tokyo", to: "Sydney"}]   ;; -> 7795.28
    -> [city-distance {from: "Atlantis", to: "Rome"}]  ;; -> #unknown-city
]
